WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
Three readings. Why is recall still hard?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
Familiarity is not evidence of recall.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
A 2006 study compared two kinds of practice.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Students studied prose, then reread or recalled.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
Immediate and delayed tests were separated.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
With the text open, answers are present.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
Close it, and you must find the path.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Missing pieces reveal what needs attention.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
Check feedback and repair the gaps.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
After five minutes, repeated study did better.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
After days, retrieval practice did better.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
Ease now and retention later can diverge.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Explain a passage with the book closed.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Check it, then try again after an interval.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Effects depend on material, feedback and timing.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Difficulty can sometimes offer a clue.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Both understanding and recall need practice.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Ask both: familiar, and retrievable?
